## Passing References to Functions

Most functions should take parameters by reference (`&T` or `&mut T`) instead of taking ownership. This keeps the caller's data alive and usable.

### Read-Only Functions: Use &T

```xiom
fn length(v: &Vec[Int]) -> Int {
  return v.len();
}

fn contains(v: &Vec[Int], target: Int) -> Bool {
  var i = 0;
  while i < v.len() {
    if v[i] == target { return true; }
    i = i + 1;
  }
  return false;
}

// Caller keeps ownership
let data = Vec[Int]();
data.push(1);
let len = length(&data);  // borrow
data.push(2);              // data still usable
```

### Modifying Functions: Use &mut T

```xiom
fn append(v: &mut Vec[Int], value: Int) -> Unit {
  v.push(value);
}

fn clear(v: &mut Vec[Int]) -> Unit {
  v.clear();
}

var data = Vec[Int]();
append(&mut data, 42);  // mutable borrow
append(&mut data, 99);  // another mutable borrow (previous ended)
```

### Struct Methods with Borrow

```xiom
pub type Point = { x: Float64; y: Float64; }

fn Point.distance(self: &Point, other: &Point) -> Float64 {
  let dx = self.x - other.x;
  let dy = self.y - other.y;
  return math.sqrt(dx * dx + dy * dy);
}
```

### When to Take Ownership

Take ownership only when the function needs to:
- Store the value somewhere
- Transform it into something else (consume)
- Transfer ownership to another thread or scope

```xiom
// Takes ownership — caller loses access
fn into_set(v: Vec[Int]) -> Set[Int] {
  let s = Set[Int]();
  for item in v {
    s.insert(item);
  }
  return s;
}
```

